{"name":"Tawny Ember","hex":"#a7947c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tawny","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a light brown to brownish orange colour; orangey brown tinged with gold."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause (someone or something) to have a light brown to brownish orange colour; to tan, to tawn.","To become a light brown to brownish orange colour; to tan, to tawn."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tawny"},{"term":"ember","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of coal or wood glowing by heat; a hot coal.","Smoldering ash."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Making a circuit of the year or the seasons; recurring in each quarter of the year, as certain religious days set apart for fasting and prayer."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ember"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ember","blocks":[{"text":"An ember, also called a hot coal, is a hot lump of smouldering solid fuel, typically glowing, composed of greatly heated wood, coal, or other carbon-based material. Embers can exist within, remain after, or sometimes precede, a fire. Embers are, in some cases, as hot as the fire which created them. They radiate a substantial amount of heat long after the fire has been extinguished, and if not taken care of properly can rekindle a fire that is thought to be completely extinguished and can pose a fire hazard. In order to avoid the danger of accidentally spreading a fire, many campers pour water on the embers or cover them in dirt. Alternatively, embers can be used to relight a fire after it has gone out without the need to rebuild the fire – in a conventional fireplace, a fire can easily be relit up to 12 hours after it goes out, provided that there is enough space for air to circulate between the embers and the introduced fuel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Embers_01.JPG/330px-Embers_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ember","wikidata":"Q177257"},{"title":"Tawny (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Tawny is a light brown to brownish-orange colour."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Natugle_stor.jpg/330px-Natugle_stor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tawny_(colour)","wikidata":"Q2836489"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Bonfire_Embers.jpg/500px-Bonfire_Embers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Bonfire_Embers.jpg/500px-Bonfire_Embers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A bonfire in rural Australia, with a large number of sparks being blown by the wind; sparks are akin to embers in a similar way that dust is to stone.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABonfire_Embers.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg/500px-Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg/1280px-Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg/500px-Hiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Embers of firewood used in sauna stove","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHiillos_Aitokiuas.jpg"}],"sounds":{"tawny":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0e/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-tawny.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/t